---
import { Picture } from 'astro:assets';
import Sheet from '../layouts/Sheet.astro';
import { href } from '../lib/url';
import '../styles/home.css';
import heygen from '../assets/img/heygen-editor.jpg';
import curio from '../assets/img/curio-garden.jpg';
import eaton from '../assets/img/eaton-rapidlinkx.jpg';
import echo from '../assets/img/echo.jpg';

const resume = 'https://drive.google.com/file/d/1XlaKtkU-4iRvNvgbC6oJTdFKX5hse0aw/view?usp=sharing';
const observe = 'https://github.com/KyleXu0420/observe';
const figSizes = '(max-width: 899px) calc(100vw - 48px), (max-width: 1023px) 48vw, 560px';
---
<Sheet
  title="Kyle Xu — AI product designer · design engineer"
  description="Kyle Xu designs AI products in the codebase they ship from. Most recently Temper (a token architecture and the design-lint checkers that keep it honest) and Dify (Droploft, built end-to-end in code)."
  sheet="00 / index"
>
  <div class="bubbles" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="br"><span class="bub">A</span></div>
    <div class="bf"><span class="bub B">B</span><span class="bub C">C</span><span class="bub D">D</span></div>
  </div>

  <!-- 00 — hero -->
  <section class="s hero" aria-labelledby="h1">
    <div class="rid">
      <div class="label"><span aria-hidden="true">// 00 — </span>kyle xu</div>
      <div class="meta mono">
        <span class="name">Kyle Xu <span class="cjk muted">徐匡泓</span></span>
        <span class="role">ai product designer · design engineer</span>
        <span class="muted pron">they / them</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="field">
      <h1 class="display-xl" id="h1">I design AI products in the codebase they ship from. <em>Not in a file that describes it.</em></h1>
      <p class="lede">Most recently at Temper — a token architecture and the design-lint checkers that keep it honest, shipped to main — and at Dify, where I built Droploft end-to-end in code with no Figma handoff. Before that I trained as an architect, which is where most of how I think comes from.</p>
      <div class="cta">
        <a class="go" href="#work">↓ selected work</a>
        <a class="go" href={observe} rel="noopener">observe on github ↗</a>
        <a class="go" href={resume} rel="noopener">resume (pdf) ↗</a>
        <a class="go" href="mailto:kylexu@umich.edu">kylexu@umich.edu</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="rnow">
      <dl>
        <dt>latest</dt><dd>Temper · design engineer (contract) · 2026.07 — 2026.09</dd>
        <dt>before</dt><dd>Dify · Curio · Infloww · Heygen · Eaton</dd>
        <dt>trained</dt><dd>HCI, University of Michigan · architecture, XAUAT</dd>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 01 — work -->
  <section class="s work" id="work" aria-labelledby="h-work">
    <div class="rail"><h2 class="label" id="h-work"><span aria-hidden="true">// 01 — </span>work</h2></div>
    <div class="field"><p class="gloss">three in full, three in short form. dates and numbers are the resume's; each row states one thing that can be checked.</p></div>
  </section>
  <div>
    <article class="row" data-case="temper">
      <div class="rr"><span class="n">01</span>Temper<span class="sep"> · </span><br />2026.07 — 2026.09<span class="sep"> · </span><br />Design Engineer (Contract)</div>
      <div class="rf">
        <div class="rt">
          <h3 class="title">A token architecture, and the checkers that keep it honest</h3>
          <p>Sole designer in the production repo. Two layers of tokens over 6 semantic hues, each with a stated meaning; a type and spacing scale from 3 utilities to 19; hardcoded colour down 60%; dark theme from partial to systematic. Then four zero-dependency checkers — 16 lint rules plus theme-parity, dead-class and doc-citation — every rule shipped with a control fixture.</p>
          <a class="go" href={observe} rel="noopener">→ the method, open-sourced as observe ↗</a>
        </div>
        <figure class="fig diagram">
          <div class="mat">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 520 300" role="img" aria-label="Detail drawing: a lint rule, the planted defect it must catch, the near-miss it must leave alone, and the second pass that breaks the rule on purpose to prove the fixture is load-bearing">
              <text x="18" y="27" class="t3">detail 1 — a rule and its control fixture</text>
              <g fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1">
                <rect x="18.5" y="56.5" width="132" height="76" />
                <rect x="196.5" y="46.5" width="150" height="42" class="accs" />
                <rect x="196.5" y="102.5" width="150" height="42" />
                <line x1="150.5" y1="82" x2="196.5" y2="67.5" />
                <line x1="150.5" y1="106" x2="196.5" y2="123.5" />
                <line x1="346.5" y1="67.5" x2="362" y2="67.5" />
                <line x1="346.5" y1="123.5" x2="362" y2="123.5" />
                <rect x="18.5" y="182.5" width="490" height="98" stroke-dasharray="3 3" />
              </g>
              <g fill="currentColor"><circle cx="150.5" cy="82" r="2" /><circle cx="150.5" cy="106" r="2" /></g>
              <text x="30" y="82">rule</text>
              <text x="30" y="104" class="t3">no raw hex</text>
              <text x="30" y="122" class="t3">css + markup</text>
              <text x="206" y="65" class="acc">planted defect</text>
              <text x="206" y="81" class="t3">color: #hex</text>
              <text x="206" y="121">near-miss</text>
              <text x="206" y="137" class="t3">color: var(--fg)</text>
              <text x="370" y="72">→ must catch</text>
              <text x="370" y="128">→ must stay quiet</text>
              <text x="30" y="206">second pass — break the rule on purpose</text>
              <text x="30" y="228" class="t3">→ the planted defect must now get through</text>
              <text x="30" y="248" class="t3">→ if it doesn't, the fixture was never load-bearing</text>
              <text x="30" y="268" class="t3">→ and the checker refuses to say “pass”</text>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <figcaption><b>FIG 01</b><span>How a rule proves it still works: the fixture, drawn. The site you are reading runs the same kind of check on itself at every build.</span></figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="row" data-case="droploft">
      <div class="rr"><span class="n">02</span>Dify (LangGenius)<span class="sep"> · </span><br />2026.02 — 2026.06<span class="sep"> · </span><br />AI Product Designer (Design Engineer)</div>
      <div class="rf">
        <div class="rt">
          <h3 class="title">Droploft, a team knowledge base built end-to-end in code</h3>
          <p>Designed and built with Claude Code — no Figma handoff. A 4-level information architecture, interactive knowledge-graph patterns, publish-ready HTML content flows, 30+ product screens, the Living Tokens design system and 8+ landing pages, partnering with engineering only at the integration layer. Also at Dify: Syncless, a 0→1 multi-agent workplace concept, and Skills Agent for Dify Enterprise.</p>
          <a class="go" href="https://www.figma.com/design/3nUKfaJoAkZQdlZwzpyFff/Dify-Demos?node-id=0-1" rel="noopener">→ figma file, for now ↗</a>
        </div>
        <figure class="fig diagram">
          <div class="mat">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 520 300" role="img" aria-label="Plan drawing of a four-level information architecture, each level nested inside the previous, with a knowledge graph in the innermost level and a publish exit to HTML">
              <g fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1">
                <rect x="10.5" y="10.5" width="499" height="279" />
                <rect x="120.5" y="44.5" width="389" height="245" />
                <rect x="230.5" y="78.5" width="279" height="211" />
                <rect x="340.5" y="112.5" width="169" height="177" />
                <g class="accs">
                  <line x1="372" y1="196" x2="422" y2="168" /><line x1="422" y1="168" x2="474" y2="206" /><line x1="422" y1="168" x2="402" y2="246" /><line x1="474" y1="206" x2="402" y2="246" /><line x1="474" y1="206" x2="486" y2="252" /><line x1="372" y1="196" x2="402" y2="246" />
                </g>
                <g fill="var(--bg-mat)" stroke="currentColor">
                  <circle cx="372" cy="196" r="5" /><circle cx="422" cy="168" r="5" /><circle cx="474" cy="206" r="5" /><circle cx="402" cy="246" r="5" /><circle cx="486" cy="252" r="5" />
                </g>
                <path d="M36 262 L36 288" stroke-dasharray="2 3" />
                <path d="M31 283 L36 289 L41 283" />
              </g>
              <text x="20" y="32">level 1</text>
              <text x="130" y="66">level 2</text>
              <text x="240" y="100">level 3</text>
              <text x="350" y="134">level 4</text>
              <text x="350" y="280" class="t3">knowledge graph</text>
              <text x="20" y="236" class="t3">publish</text>
              <text x="20" y="252" class="t3">→ html</text>
              <text x="500" y="32" class="t3" text-anchor="end">4 levels · 30+ screens</text>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <figcaption><b>FIG 02</b><span>Droploft's four-level information architecture, drawn as a plan. Product screens follow once Dify has cleared them.</span></figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="row" data-case="heygen">
      <div class="rr"><span class="n">03</span>Heygen<span class="sep"> · </span><br />2023.06 — 2023.10<span class="sep"> · </span><br />Product Design Intern</div>
      <div class="rf">
        <div class="rt">
          <h3 class="title"><a href={href('/projects/heygen')}>Re-architecting the video editor around the script</a></h3>
          <p>Reorganised the editor around script, canvas and timeline with an updated component library: engagement +32.1% and task completion +24.3%, measured against the flow it replaced rather than a fresh baseline. Then a 0→1 iOS companion MVP in a 3-week sprint.</p>
          <a class="go" href={href('/projects/heygen')}>→ read the case</a>
        </div>
        <figure class="fig">
          <div class="mat"><Picture src={heygen} formats={['avif', 'webp']} widths={[560, 800, 1200]} sizes={figSizes} alt="Heygen video editor after the re-architecture: script panel on the left, avatar canvas in the centre, timeline below" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></div>
          <figcaption><b>FIG 03</b><span>The editor after the re-architecture — script in the sidebar, canvas fixed, timeline as its own panel. 2023.</span></figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="row sup" data-case="curio">
      <div class="rr"><span class="n">04</span>Curio Digital Therapeutics<span class="sep"> · </span><br />2024.11 — 2025.12<span class="sep"> · </span><br />Founding Product Designer<br /><span class="tag">short form</span></div>
      <div class="rf">
        <div class="rt">
          <h3 class="title">MamaLift &amp; MenoLift, FDA-cleared digital therapeutics</h3>
          <p>First designer on the team, under clinical review from the first screen: the design system and information architecture from zero, and the Achievement Garden — a gamified engagement framework with 100+ original assets.</p>
          <a class="go" href="https://www.figma.com/design/BiXcn2d7kNwzXtuw1aUGDx/Curio-Work?node-id=0-1" rel="noopener">→ figma file, for now ↗</a>
        </div>
        <figure class="fig">
          <div class="mat"><Picture src={curio} formats={['avif', 'webp']} widths={[560, 800, 1200]} sizes={figSizes} alt="Four phone screens from the Achievement Garden: a daily garden, a relationship pool, a progress ring and a rewards shop" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></div>
          <figcaption><b>FIG 04</b><span>The Achievement Garden, four of its screens.</span></figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="row sup" data-case="eaton">
      <div class="rr"><span class="n">05</span>Eaton Corporation<span class="sep"> · </span><br />2022.06 — 2022.08<span class="sep"> · </span><br />UX Design Intern<br /><span class="tag">short form</span></div>
      <div class="rf">
        <div class="rt">
          <h3 class="title"><a href={href('/projects/eaton')}>RapidLinkX, an IoT configuration tool for airports</a></h3>
          <p>Parameter editing and error monitoring for airport conveyor systems: operational efficiency +49.7% and error rate −36.8% in usability testing; 14 component variants added to Eaton's design system.</p>
          <a class="go" href={href('/projects/eaton')}>→ read the case</a>
        </div>
        <figure class="fig">
          <div class="mat"><Picture src={eaton} formats={['avif', 'webp']} widths={[560, 800, 1200]} sizes={figSizes} alt="RapidLinkX parameter table: linked devices on the left, a table of motor parameters with ranges and defaults" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></div>
          <figcaption><b>FIG 05</b><span>RapidLinkX, parameter editing view.</span></figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="row sup" data-case="echo">
      <div class="rr"><span class="n">06</span>University of Michigan<span class="sep"> · </span><br />M.S. thesis · 2022 — 2025<span class="sep"> · </span><br /><span class="tag">research</span></div>
      <div class="rf">
        <div class="rt">
          <h3 class="title"><a href="https://doi.org/10.7302/29019" rel="noopener">Echo, a future-self avatar for behaviour change</a></h3>
          <p>An LLM-driven conversational agent and avatar interface for everyday health behaviour, evaluated in a 16-day pilot with 8 participants and a 12-day deployment with 36 users. It is why I'm sceptical of AI interfaces that are only demoed, never used.</p>
          <a class="go" href="https://doi.org/10.7302/29019" rel="noopener">→ the thesis on Deep Blue ↗</a>
        </div>
        <figure class="fig">
          <div class="mat"><Picture src={echo} formats={['avif', 'webp']} widths={[560, 800]} sizes={figSizes} alt="Two phone screens from Echo: an avatar introducing itself and a conversation view" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></div>
          <figcaption><b>FIG 06</b><span>Echo's avatar and conversation views.</span></figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="row also">
      <div class="rr"><span class="n">also</span>Infloww<span class="sep"> · </span><br />2024.03 — 2024.11<span class="sep"> · </span><br />Product Designer (AI Specialist)</div>
      <div class="rf">
        <p>Designed and shipped an AI chat assistant MVP at 91% user satisfaction, and payment and mass-messaging tools that saved 37 minutes per outreach session — partnering with prompt engineers on the cases where the model is wrong, not only the demo path. No public capture; no case page.</p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>

  <!-- 02 — method -->
  <section class="s method" id="method" aria-labelledby="h-method">
    <div class="rail"><h2 class="label" id="h-method"><span aria-hidden="true">// 02 — </span>method</h2><p class="gloss">three claims, each with something to click.</p></div>
    <div class="field">
      <div class="cols">
        <div class="m">
          <span class="dbub" aria-hidden="true">1</span>
          <h3 class="h3">I ship design as production code.</h3>
          <p>At Temper the vocabulary existed and the code wasn't speaking it. I rebuilt the foundation as a two-layer token architecture and took it to main — code to Figma rather than the reverse. At Dify I designed and built Droploft end-to-end in code.</p>
          <a class="go" href="#work">→ Temper · Droploft</a>
        </div>
        <div class="m">
          <span class="dbub" aria-hidden="true">2</span>
          <h3 class="h3">I make design decisions checkable.</h3>
          <p>A rule that only lives in a document drifts. So I wrote four zero-dependency checkers — 16 lint rules plus theme parity, dead classes and doc citations. Every rule ships with a control fixture: a planted defect it must catch, a near-miss it must leave alone. A second pass breaks each rule on purpose to prove the fixtures hold.</p>
          <a class="go" href="#tools">→ the method, open-sourced as observe</a>
        </div>
        <div class="m">
          <span class="dbub" aria-hidden="true">3</span>
          <h3 class="h3">I've studied how people actually use AI.</h3>
          <p>Echo, my Michigan thesis: a future-self avatar driven by an LLM, built to shift everyday health behaviour, run as a 16-day pilot with 8 participants and a 12-day deployment with 36. At Infloww I designed the assistant for the cases where the model is wrong.</p>
          <a class="go" href="https://doi.org/10.7302/29019" rel="noopener">→ Echo, on Deep Blue ↗</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 03 — tools -->
  <section class="s" id="tools" aria-labelledby="h-tools">
    <div class="rail"><h2 class="label" id="h-tools"><span aria-hidden="true">// 03 — </span>tools</h2><p class="gloss">the proof that isn't self-reported: a repo you can clone.</p></div>
    <div class="field">
      <div class="spec">
        <div class="sl">
          <h3 class="nm">observe <i>open source</i></h3>
          <p>Tools for finding out how you and your coding agents actually work, from the transcripts you already have. Eight tools, Python 3.9, no dependencies. Every one runs a control before it reports — a planted case it must get right, and a clean case it must stay quiet on — and <code class="mono">mutate</code> breaks each tool on purpose to prove the controls notice.</p>
          <p>It grew out of the Temper design-lint: the rules there needed control fixtures to stay trustworthy, and the method became a standalone harness.</p>
          <a class="go" href={observe} rel="noopener">→ github.com/KyleXu0420/observe ↗</a>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dl>
            <div><dt>language</dt><dd>Python 3.9 · 0 dependencies</dd></div>
            <div><dt>licence</dt><dd>MIT</dd></div>
            <div><dt>tools</dt><dd>8, plus <code class="mono">mutate</code></dd></div>
            <div><dt>selfcheck</dt><dd>8 / 8 controls ok · 2026-09-13</dd></div>
            <div><dt>origin</dt><dd>the Temper design-lint checkers, 2026</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 04 — origin -->
  <section class="s origin" id="origin" aria-labelledby="h-origin">
    <div class="rail"><h2 class="label" id="h-origin"><span aria-hidden="true">// 04 — </span>origin</h2></div>
    <div class="field">
      <p class="lede">I trained as an architect — six years, undergraduate through a degree in urban renovation. Architecture is where I learned to think in constraints: a building holds because someone decided what the parts are and what each one is allowed to do, and wrote it down in a way that can be checked. A design system is the same problem in a different material.</p>
      <a class="go" href={href('/about')}>→ about</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Sheet>
